  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nBy late monday night/early Tuesday morning another upper level\ntrough will push inland with models now showing the core of this\nnext system lifting to the NE through NV and UT. However the\nbottom of the trough will move through Arizona providing enough\nascent to the region to increase PoPs back to between 60-90% \nacross South-Central AZ, and 20-60% across SW AZ. PWATs Tuesday \nwill also increase to over 200%, with the latest WPC QPF \nindicating Tuesday's rain totals between 0.25\"-0.50\" across the \nGreater Phoenix area, locally higher in thunderstorms, and close \nto 1.00\" over Central AZ higher terrain to the north/northeast. \nHowever, considerable spread remains in the model guidance, with \n25th-75th percentile QPF ranging between a few tenths to upwards \nof 1.25\", focused over the higher terrain to the north/northeast \nof Phoenix. A 1-2 day break in the unsettled weather will then \nlikely ensue following Tuesday, ahead of the next system. \n\nDuring the latter half of the week, ensembles are pointing \ntowards yet another upper level trough moving inland from the \nPacific NW, however, there are still considerable discrepancies in\nthe trajectory of this followup system (how far south it will \npropagate, which will impact the moisture return and placement of\nthe best upper level ascent mechanisms). Due to this, confidence \nremains low for PoPs/QPF late in the work week, but at the very \nleast, there is building consensus for an even cooler airmass to \nenter the region as a result of this second system. Latest NBM \nhighs continue to trend towards the 60s to lower 70s by late week.\nHowever, the spread in the model guidance increases considerably \non Friday, with IQRs on the NBM forecast temperatures increasing \nto 8F-10F.",
